            "text": "An optimal and memory-efficient solution in Python to compute the sum of Euclidean distances over all ordered pairs. \n\nTo avoid the out-of-memory error caused by materializing full pairwise difference tensors, this implementation converts the vectors to `float64` for numerical precision and computes the distances in row chunks using matrix multiplication ($\\|x_i - x_j\\|^2 = \\|x_i\\|^2 + \\|x_j\\|^2 - 2 x_i \\cdot x_j$). This takes advantage of highly optimized BLAS GEMM while maintaining a small memory footprint (~200 MB).\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n\ndef main():\n    # Load data without modifying the source file\n    X = np.load(\"vectors_secondary.npy\").astype(np.float64)\n    n = X.shape[0]\n\n    # Precompute squared norms for each vector\n    sq_norms = np.sum(X**2, axis=1)\n\n    total_dist = 0.0\n    chunk_size = 1000\n\n    for i in range(0, n, chunk_size):\n        chunk_len = min(chunk_size, n - i)\n        X_chunk = X[i : i + chunk_len]\n\n        # Dot product via BLAS GEMM: shape (chunk_len, n)\n        G_chunk = X_chunk @ X.T\n\n        # Pairwise squared distances: ||u - v||^2 = ||u||^2 + ||v||^2 - 2<u, v>\n        d2 = sq_norms[i : i + chunk_len, None] + sq_norms[None, :] - 2.0 * G_chunk\n\n        # Ensure diagonal elements are strictly zero\n        for k in range(chunk_len):\n            d2[k, i + k] = 0.0\n\n        # Guard against small negative roundoff errors before square root\n        np.maximum(d2, 0.0, out=d2)\n        np.sqrt(d2, out=d2)\n\n        total_dist += float(np.sum(d2))\n\n    print(f\"TOTAL:{total_dist}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
